{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And the chief butler spoke, etc., saying, etc.</b> The reason he said <b>saying</b> — and he also said <b>et</b> and did not say אל. And perhaps he did not speak to him directly but rather to the wise men of Egypt, to say to Pharaoh, and therefore you will find that all his words are in a covert manner, not face to face — ״Pharaoh was wrathful and he placed״ is in the covert tense." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend in his saying <b>saying et my sins</b> along these lines: that you should not think I remember what befell me through the king's punishments — on the contrary, I declare my lowliness and deficiency, for I am the sinner. And that is his saying <b>saying et my sins</b>, etc. And he was precise in saying <b>today</b>, for it is the time of need, since otherwise he would have concealed the memory of his petty deeds. In this too he gave a reason why he did not report the matter beforehand, given the obligation incumbent upon the king's intimates to report such a novel matter as this — for there was no reason to recall his offenses further. And he said <b>my sins</b> in the plural, to say that what they had already made into many sins was significant in his eyes. Or perhaps he intended to allude also to this sin — that he withheld the information until now; therefore he used the plural ״my sins.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:30.079521376Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}